      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Stress induced analgesia (SIA) and stress-induced changes in body temperature were studied in mice and rats. Immobilization was used as the stressor. Nociception was measured with the tail-flick method and body temperature was recorded in the colon. Within 5 min of immobilization a similar increase in tail-flick latencies was observed in the two species. Concomitantly, the body temperature increased in the rats and decreased in the mice. Transection of the spinal cord 7 days before the experiments tended to increase the effect of stress on the tail-flick latencies in both species. Pretreatment with naloxone HCL (2 mg/kg SC, 5 min before immobilization) did not influence SIA in either intact or spinal rats. Thus, analgesia induced by immobilization may be due to a non-opioid substance acting peripherally or reaching the spinal cord via the systemic circulation."},
